  { q: "Why is the Mid-Autumn Festival important to Chinese families?", a: "It's a major reunion festival. Families gather under the full moon, share mooncakes, and celebrate being together — the round, full moon stands for wholeness and family unity. It matters because it's a time to come home." },
  { q: "Is the Mid-Autumn Festival the same as Chinese Thanksgiving?", a: "Not really. Calling it 'Chinese Thanksgiving' is misleading — the emphasis is reunion and the moon, not a specific harvest-feast myth from another culture. It's best understood on its own terms." },
  { q: "What is the story of Chang'e?", a: "Chang'e is a woman associated with the moon. In one common version she rises to the moon after taking an elixir — but the legend has several versions with different details, so it's best treated as folklore rather than a single fixed story." },
  { q: "When is the Mid-Autumn Festival?", a: "It falls on the 15th day of the 8th month of the Chinese lunar calendar, when the moon is full — so its date on the Western calendar shifts each year, usually landing in September or early October." },
  { q: "What do people eat during the festival?", a: "Mooncakes (月饼) are the signature food — round pastries, often filled with sweet-bean or lotus-seed paste, shared and cut so everyone gets a piece. Families also gather for a larger reunion meal." },
